-- WARNING: COPY ------------- errors/copy_shared_3.march

`bump` builds a new copy instead of reusing `t` in place, because `t` is used again afterwards. [copy_shared]
A function can update a value in place only when it holds the only reference to it; make this call the last use of `t` and `bump` reuses its memory instead of allocating.

29 |     let u = bump(t)
                      ^


    `t` is used again here, so it is still shared when `bump` runs:

30 |     let a = size(t)
                      ^
    run `march --explain copy_shared`


